WEBVTT

00:00:00.141 --> 00:00:03.412
This slide asks you to diagnose a poorly designed everything table.

00:00:03.412 --> 00:00:07.457
Start with identity. There is no stable primary key, and names are not reliable identifiers.

00:00:07.457 --> 00:00:09.382
Next, look at row grain.

00:00:09.382 --> 00:00:12.847
Patient, provider, appointment, services, and charges are all mixed together.

00:00:12.847 --> 00:00:16.802
Then inspect types. Time and money are stored as text, making validation and calculation harder.

00:00:16.802 --> 00:00:20.072
The services column also contains a list instead of one relational value.

00:00:20.072 --> 00:00:26.217
Before revealing the repair direction, try to name at least three problems yourself.

00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:27.917
The goal is not merely to split the table because normalization says so.

00:00:27.917 --> 00:00:32.782
Explain which fact belongs to which entity, which identifiers need protection,

00:00:32.782 --> 00:00:35.287
and which rules should become database constraints.

00:00:35.287 --> 00:00:39.492
Connect this idea to a clinic system separating patients, encounters, clinicians, and billing.

00:00:39.492 --> 00:00:42.027
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:42.027 --> 00:00:49.107
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:49.107 --> 00:00:51.207
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:51.207 --> 00:00:56.173
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
